DATABASE documentation, last updated on 11 Sep 2026

A module that needs a database asks the server for one.  It does not link
against libpq, it does not learn what a PGresult is, and it does not get a
thread of its own: it calls db_query(), and some time later its callback
runs in the main thread with the rows as JSON.

This is the implementation of that idea.  The API is include/db.h; the
dispatcher behind it is ircd/db.c; the thing that actually opens a socket
to PostgreSQL is modules/workers/postgres/, which is a module like any
other and is loaded, unloaded and reloaded like any other.


READ THIS FIRST
===============

Three things are true of every query, and everything else follows:

  A QUERY NEVER BLOCKS THE SERVER.

db_query() hands the work to a connection thread and returns immediately.
The answer arrives later -- a different pass through the event loop, and up
to the configured timeout later.  Nothing in the API waits, because the
core is single-threaded and a core that waits on a database is a core that
has stopped serving its users.

  A QUERY NEVER OUTLIVES FIVE SECONDS.

The timeout is configurable and it is capped at five seconds, in the
configuration parser and again in the driver.  A configuration asking for
thirty is corrected to five, with a warning.  The cap holds even when the
database stops answering entirely: the driver waits on poll() against its
own deadline, not on libpq, so a TCP connection to a machine that has gone
away is cut off on time rather than minutes later.

  A VALUE NEVER BECOMES SQL.

Statements are prepared, values are bound.  There is no entry point in this
API that takes an assembled statement and no code path in the driver that
concatenates a value into one.  A parameter containing "'; drop table
accounts; --" is a string that matches nothing.


THE SHAPE OF IT
===============

    a module                 the ircd                  the postgres module
    --------                 --------                  -------------------
    db_query(mod, q, cb, u)
                          -> remembers the call
                             as an id, hands the
                             id to the driver
                                                    -> copies the query into
                                                       worker memory, queues
                                                       it on the read pool

                                                       ... connection thread:
                                                       prepare, execute, wait
                                                       on a deadline, build
                                                       the rows as JSON ...

                                                    <- worker_post()
                          <- db_complete(id, ...)
    cb(result, u)         <- ... unless the module
                             was unloaded meanwhile

The ircd in the middle is not ceremony.  Modules are opened with
RTLD_LOCAL, so a database API living in the driver module would have no
callers at all -- no other module could resolve its symbols.  Putting the
API in the ircd, which exports its own, is what makes it reachable.  And
holding the callbacks there is what makes it safe to unload a module with
queries in flight: the calls are forgotten, and the answer that arrives two
seconds later finds nothing to call.


USING IT
========

Read:

    static void account_loaded(const struct DbResult* res, void* user)
    {
      struct Client* sptr;
      json_t* row;

      if (res->err.dberr_code != DB_OK) {
        log_write(LS_SYSTEM, L_ERROR, 0, "lookup failed: %s",
                  res->err.dberr_message);
        return;
      }

      if (!(sptr = findNUser((const char*) user)))
        return;                 /* they left while we were asking */

      if (!(row = json_array_get(res->data, 0)))
        return;                 /* no such account */

      sendcmdto_one(&me, CMD_NOTICE, sptr, "%C :score %lld", sptr,
                    (long long) json_integer_value(json_object_get(row,
                                                                   "score")));
    }

    struct DbParam  nick  = { DB_TYPE_TEXT, cli_name(sptr), DB_FORMAT_TEXT };
    struct DbParam* args[] = { &nick, NULL };
    struct DbQuery  query = { "select score from accounts where nick = $1",
                              args };

    if (db_query(mod, &query, account_loaded, numnick) != DB_OK)
      send_reply(sptr, ERR_UNAVAILRESOURCE, "accounts", "try again later");

Write, which is the same call on the other pool:

    struct DbParam  nick  = { DB_TYPE_TEXT, cli_name(sptr), DB_FORMAT_TEXT };
    struct DbParam  score = { DB_TYPE_INT, "1", DB_FORMAT_TEXT };
    struct DbParam* args[] = { &nick, &score, NULL };
    struct DbQuery  query = { "update accounts set score = score + $2"
                              " where nick = $1", args };

    db_exec(mod, &query, NULL, NULL);    /* nobody needs the answer */

Four rules for the caller, and they are all about time passing:

  - THE QUERY IS COPIED, THE RESULT IS NOT.  Everything in the DbQuery may
    be a local variable; it is copied before db_query() returns.  The
    DbResult is the driver's and is released as soon as the callback
    returns: keep the rows with json_incref() if they are needed later.

  - DO NOT CAPTURE A struct Client*.  Seconds pass.  A client that quits is
    returned to a free list and its memory handed to somebody else, so a
    pointer held across a query is a use-after-free waiting for a slow
    database.  Store the numnick and look it up again, exactly as a worker
    task does; see include/worker.h.

  - CHECK THE RETURN VALUE.  DB_OK means the query was accepted and the
    callback will run.  Anything else is a refusal here and now -- no
    driver loaded, no configuration, workers off, pool busy, malformed
    parameters -- and the callback will never run.

  - THE CALLBACK MAY NOT RUN.  If the module is unloaded first, its
    callbacks are dropped.  That is deliberate, and it is why a module does
    not have to drain the database before it can be unloaded.


WHAT COMES BACK
===============

DbResult::data is a JSON array with one object per row, mapping column name
to value.  A statement that returns nothing -- an INSERT, an UPDATE -- gets
an empty array rather than NULL, so nothing needs a special case, and
DbResult::rows carries how many rows it touched.

The type mapping is deliberately conservative:

    integers (int2/int4/int8)     JSON integer
    float4, float8, numeric       JSON number
    bool                          JSON true/false
    json, jsonb                   the document itself, embedded
    NULL                          JSON null
    everything else               JSON string

"Everything else" is timestamps, UUIDs, inet, arrays, the \x form of a
bytea, and any type nobody has thought about.  A string is never wrong;
guessing at a structure would be.  Values the server prints in a form JSON
has no spelling for -- a numeric NaN or Infinity -- stay strings too.


WHAT GOES WRONG
===============

DbErrDetails carries a code from a closed enum and a message.  Neither is
PostgreSQL's: there is no SQLSTATE in the code and no libpq text in the
message.  The codes are in include/db.h; the ones worth handling
individually are

    DB_ERR_UNIQUE        a record with that value already exists
    DB_ERR_FOREIGN_KEY   a referenced record does not exist
    DB_ERR_NOT_NULL      a required value was missing
    DB_ERR_RETRY         deadlock or serialization failure; running the
                         same statement again is the correct response
    DB_ERR_TIMEOUT       it took longer than the configured timeout
    DB_ERR_BUSY          every connection is busy and the queue is full
    DB_ERR_UNAVAILABLE   no driver, or no worker threads

The message is the driver's own wording and is safe to show a user.  That
is the reason it is not the database's: a PostgreSQL error string names
tables, columns, constraints and often a fragment of the statement, and a
module that put it in a NOTICE would be handing the schema to whoever asked
for it.  The database's own account is not lost -- it goes to the server
log, where the operator can read it and nobody else can.


CONFIGURING IT
==============

    Database {
      dsn   = "host=db.example.org dbname=ircu user=ircu password=secret";
      read  = "host=replica.example.org dbname=ircu user=ircu";
      write = "host=db.example.org dbname=ircu user=ircu password=secret";
      pool = 4;
      read_pool = 8;
      write_pool = 2;
      timeout = 2 seconds;      # or timeout_ms = 1500;
    };

    Module { name = "postgres"; };

Only "dsn" is required, and it is the fallback for both roles: a server
with no replica sets it and nothing else.  db_query() goes to the read
role, db_exec() to the write role, and each role has its own pool.

The block is read and kept whether or not a driver is loaded, which is what
lets the Module block appear before it, after it, or not at all.

The timeout is capped at DB_TIMEOUT_MAX_MS -- five seconds -- and so is
everything derived from it, including the connect.  "timeout" takes the
usual time units; "timeout_ms" is the same knob in milliseconds, for a
deadline shorter than a second.

The driver needs worker threads, because that is where all of its waiting
happens.  WORKER_THREADS is zero by default and every query is refused
until it is set; set it to at least the total size of the pools.  See
doc/readme.workers.


BUILDING IT
===========

The ircd itself has no new dependencies.  libpq and jansson are needed only
by modules/workers/postgres, which declares them in its own CMake fragment
(modules/workers/postgres/module.cmake) rather than in the core's build
files, and which is simply not built where they are missing:

    -- Module postgres: not built (libpq and jansson not found)

Install them and configure again:

    Debian, Ubuntu     apt install libpq-dev libjansson-dev
    Red Hat, Fedora    dnf install libpq-devel jansson-devel
    FreeBSD            pkg install postgresql16-client jansson

That fragment is also the pattern for any other module with dependencies of
its own; cmake/IrcuModules.cmake documents what it may set.


INSIDE THE DRIVER
=================

modules/workers/postgres/ is one module in six files, and postgres.h says
which thread each of them runs in.

    postgres.c    the module: registers the driver, and nothing else
    pg_pool.c     the queue, the pools, and all of the copying
    pg_conn.c     one connection, its statement cache, and its deadline
    pg_json.c     PGresult -> json_t
    pg_error.c    SQLSTATE -> enum DbError, and the wording
    pg_types.c    enum DbType -> PostgreSQL OID

One pooled connection is one dedicated worker thread (worker_spawn(), via
module_spawn_worker()).  It owns its PGconn outright -- no connection is
ever touched by two threads -- and it obeys the rule in doc/readme.workers
without exception: worker_alloc() for memory, worker_log() for logging,
worker_post() to hand the answer back, and nothing else from the core at
all.  The pools start the first time a query needs one, so a server that
never runs a query never opens a connection.

Every statement is prepared before it is run, and the prepared statement is
cached per connection under the text and the parameter types it was
prepared with.  The cache is not evicted one entry at a time: when it fills
(128 statements), the connection is closed and remade, which discards every
statement prepared on it at once.  That is deliberate, and the reason is
that emptying it any other way would mean sending a DEALLOCATE -- one
statement that cannot itself go through the prepared path.  A module that
fills the cache is generating statement text per call and should not be;
the log says so when it happens.

The deadline is enforced twice.  The driver arms its own, on the monotonic
clock, and every wait it does is a poll() against it: a connect that does
not complete, a result that does not arrive, a send buffer that does not
drain.  When it passes, the query is cancelled, the connection is drained,
and the caller is told DB_ERR_TIMEOUT.  The server is told the same figure
at connect time, as statement_timeout, so a query that survives a lost
cancel still ends on its own.

For that to be possible the round trip uses libpq's non-blocking entry
points -- PQsendPrepare and PQsendQueryPrepared, which are PQprepare and
PQexecPrepared with the waiting left to the caller -- rather than the
blocking ones.  Nothing else about the prepared-statement path changes;
PQexec, which takes a statement as a string, is not used anywhere and has
no way into this driver.


UNLOADING IT
============

/MODULE UNLOAD postgres stops every connection thread, waits for whatever
they are in the middle of -- bounded by the timeout, so at most five
seconds -- fails everything still queued with DB_ERR_UNAVAILABLE, and
withdraws the driver.  Queries submitted afterwards are refused with the
same code until a driver is loaded again.

/REHASH with a changed Database block stops the pools; the next query
builds them again from the new configuration.  An unchanged block changes
nothing and costs nothing.


MIGRATIONS
==========

Schema changes are not queries and do not go through db_query(): their SQL
is DDL, which no database prepares, and a migration is a script rather than
one statement.  They have their own entry point (db_migrate()), their own
connection, their own transaction and their own timeout, and an operator
drives them by hand with /MODULE MIGRATION.  See doc/readme.migrations.


WRITING ANOTHER DRIVER
======================

Nothing in include/db.h is about PostgreSQL.  A driver is a module that
fills in a struct DbDriver -- a name, a submit and a release -- registers it
with db_register_driver() from its mi_init, and calls db_complete() when it
has an answer.  There is one driver at a time: a second registration is
refused rather than replacing the first, so two database modules in
ircd.conf produce an error in the log instead of a coin flip.
